#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "89e24005-6d6b-5631-9c24-a6eae4e468ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7111" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ at $x = -2?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x)=f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x)&=\\dfrac{1}{x}\\\n\t\tf'(x)&=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\\n\t\tf''(x)&=\\dfrac{2}{x^3}\n\t\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate $f''(x)$ at $x=-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf''(-2)=\\dfrac{2}{(-2)^3}=-\\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+2)^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}&=\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}=-\\dfrac{1}{8}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100689 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "feed91bc-ae9e-56db-9b73-a4a4f50a5c89" :question/math-academy-id "e-737" :question/problem "Find the second degree Taylor polynomial of the function $f(x) = e^{2x}$ about $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_2(x) = f(1) + f'(1)(x-1) +\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(1)(x-1)^2.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)\\mathbin{;}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & f(1) & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(1) & =2{e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(1) & =4{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1),$ $f'(1),$ and $f''(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+2{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plot below shows $f(x),$ $P_2(x),$ and $L(x).$ We can see that $P_2(x)$ is a better approximation to $f(x)$ around $x=1$ compared with $L(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030220/assets/a72eb0b9a50cdc0f8867b6f86e9273fb86502ad6060abffc34f32ae536722ac7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "690f4b9b-08e0-5001-831f-38afd7679f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}+{e}^{-1}(x-1)+4{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292775} {:answer/id #uuid "4f68150c-8047-5d90-b5a5-7456a249a4d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}+{e}^{-1}(x-1)-2{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292776} {:answer/id #uuid "07d13d08-e4d5-5bcf-930a-32005dd5dee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+2{e}^{-1}(x-1)+4{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292777} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed9f3c4-e5f4-5332-afb9-7d578d2f4aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}-2{e}^{-1}(x-1)+{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292778} {:answer/id #uuid "a873edc5-9a5e-5f2a-b8dd-36cdb60981c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{e}^{-1}(x-1)+{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed9f3c4-e5f4-5332-afb9-7d578d2f4aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}-2{e}^{-1}(x-1)+{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292778} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da855d0-36f2-52d2-af98-36a5849f3c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4baec964-433a-5f77-8daa-91b260dd4089" :question/math-academy-id "q-13817" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ about $x=1$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(1)(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(1),$ ${f}^{′}(1),$ and ${f}^{″}(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}} & ⟹ &  & f(1) & ={e}^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\,2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(1) & =-2{e}^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(1) & =2{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1),$ ${f}^{′}(1),$ and ${f}^{″}(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={e}^{-1}+(-2{e}^{-1})\\cdot (x-1)+(2{e}^{-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-2{e}^{-1}(x-1)+{e}^{-1}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f6235ce-c890-56e8-9b99-8cc51bd6188b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098455} {:answer/id #uuid "a133be0c-22ab-557e-978b-9407c28f431f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098456} {:answer/id #uuid "a603cb78-65e2-55ed-b942-00dad4565b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098457} {:answer/id #uuid "be500430-84e3-5b2c-9378-6020f40fac31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098458} {:answer/id #uuid "dce7c59e-9741-5e17-bde8-500b828a495a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be500430-84e3-5b2c-9378-6020f40fac31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}e^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098458} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4764b9-88ae-50c2-bc6c-22cffd9b554f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098454}] :question/id #uuid "37a13c97-28c5-587c-af4a-4c24db0bc1c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15426" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x + 1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = e^{3x}$ at $x =-1$?"}]}
